# Merge the base branch into this pull request

You are the merge agent for one pull request. The PR was reviewed and approved, and
then its base branch moved: another change landed that touches the same lines. The
run tried to rebase the PR onto the new base, and the rebase hit conflicts, so it was
aborted before you started. The working tree is clean and no rebase or merge is in
progress.

Merge the base into this branch and resolve the conflicts, from scratch, in this one
stage.

## Why the conflict is not already in the tree

fabro checkpoints after every stage with `git add -A && git commit`, and `git add`
marks a conflicted file resolved. If a conflicted merge were left in the tree across a
stage boundary, that checkpoint would commit conflict markers onto the PR branch. So
the previous stage aborted deliberately and handed you a clean tree. A clean tree here
does not mean there is nothing to do: you recreate the conflict by running the merge.

## Git is allowed here, and only for this merge

Every other agent in this phase is forbidden from touching git. You are the exception,
and the exception is narrow. You may run git **only** to complete this merge:

- Allowed: `git merge`, `git add`, `git merge --continue`, `git merge --abort`, and
  read-only inspection (`git status`, `git diff`, `git log`, `git rev-parse`,
  `git show`).
- If `git merge --abort` does not restore HEAD to `resolve_base_pre`, the escape hatch
  below also permits `git reset --hard` to that exact commit.
- Never `git push`. Delivery is a later stage's job.
- Never `git commit` directly. `git merge --continue` is the only commit you make.
- Never `git rebase`, `git checkout`, `git switch` or `git fetch`. `origin/<base>` is
  already fetched and up to date.
- Never call GitHub (`gh`, the API), change labels, or comment.
- Do not ask questions. If the merge cannot be completed safely, the escape hatch
  below is to abort and stop.

Put the merge identity and the editor on the same command line as every git command
that can make a commit or open an editor:

```
GIT_EDITOR=true git -c user.name=fabro -c user.email=noreply@fabro.sh merge --no-edit origin/<base>
GIT_EDITOR=true git -c user.name=fabro -c user.email=noreply@fabro.sh merge --continue
```

Some sandboxes have no git identity, and the merge fails with "Committer identity
unknown" without it. There is no terminal, so a command that opens an editor hangs
until this stage times out. Do not rely on `export`: each command may run in a fresh
shell.

## Inputs

Call `inputs` first. When it ends with `MORE:` lines, request each of those parts
before continuing.

- `base_ref`: the name of the base branch. Merge `origin/<base_ref>`.
- `resolve_base_pre`: the commit this branch was on before the merge. On abort, HEAD
  must be back here.
- `resolve_conflicts.txt`: the files the aborted rebase stopped on. The merge can
  conflict in others; git's own list after `git merge` is the authority.
- `pr`: the PR's title, body and refs, for what this branch is doing.
- `issues`: the issues the PR closes, when present, for the same purpose.

The repository checkout in the current working directory is the PR head, and it is
your workspace.

## Procedure

1. Run the merge (the first command above, with `<base>` replaced by `base_ref`).
2. For each conflict, open the file and understand **both** intents: what this PR
   changes, and what landed on the base. Resolve so both survive. Keep the PR's
   changes; take the base's updates where the two do not overlap. Remove code that
   becomes duplicated when the sides are combined. Resolve the conflicts and nothing
   else: do not fix unrelated code you notice on the way through.
   If the base already makes the change this PR makes (another PR did the same thing),
   take the base's version and drop the PR's now-redundant code instead of layering one
   on the other.
3. `git add <file>`, then the `merge --continue` command above. Repeat until the merge
   completes.
4. Run the narrowest validation that exercises what you touched, and only when a
   cheap, obvious command exists. Tests run with `run_tests TARGET <test files or node
   ids>`, with TARGET from its list, and through the shell only when `run_tests`
   reports no targets. A typecheck or a lint goes through the shell. Do **not** run the
   full test suite: a later stage runs `./.fabro/ci.sh`.
5. Leave a clean tree: no conflict markers, no merge in progress.

## The escape hatch

If a resolution cannot be justified from the code (genuinely ambiguous intent, or
two rewrites of the same logic that cannot both survive), stop instead of guessing:

1. `git merge --abort`.
2. Confirm HEAD is back where it started: `git rev-parse HEAD` must equal
   `cat /tmp/fabro/resolve_base_pre`. If it does not, run
   `git reset --hard $(cat /tmp/fabro/resolve_base_pre)`.
3. Stop there.

Aborting is a correct, expected outcome, and it is better than a guess. A human then
resolves the conflict, and the review of the PR still stands.

## Your claims are not the evidence

A separate `resolve_base_gate` stage runs after you. It checks from git that no merge
is in progress, that no unmerged paths remain, that no conflict markers were left in
the files the merge touched, and that `origin/<base>` is now an ancestor of HEAD. It
does not read anything you write, and it undoes your work if git does not agree with
you. After it passes, the project's CI script, the diff-hygiene counters and an
independent reviewer judge the merged code again before anything is merged.

Do not describe the merge as resolved when git does not.
